s at move 23 settled it. The late moves from move 29 on changed nothing, and the game ended at -16.0.", "moments": [{"turn": 9, "text": "E4 lost 3.5 points (-2.7 → -6.2) and extended Black's row of stones under White's D5/E5 pair. The engine preferred C6, and from here Black's group kept running along the fourth row."}, {"turn": 11, "text": "C4 lost 7.0 points (-4.6 → -11.7), the first real blunder. It lengthened the fourth-row line while White held D5 and E5 on top. The engine wanted G7, out in open space."}, {"turn": 18, "text": "The 20k's A4 lost 11.0 points (-11.3 → -0.3), the biggest swing of the game. After it Black trailed by only 0.3, though the board shows nothing like a capture. The engine's choice there was H6."}, {"turn": 19, "text": "H4 lost 9.3 points (-0.3 → -9.6), handing back almost everything the 20k had just given away. The engine's choice was H7. Black's wall on row 4 now stretched from B4 to H4, still under the White stones on row 5."}, {"turn": 23, "text": "D8 lost 9.6 points (-7.8 → -17.3), GPT-6.1 Sol's worst move of the game. It sat beside E8 up on the eighth row, and the engine's choice was H7. The 20k's C8 and F8 stones went on to hem in the D8–E8 pair, and the score never came back."}]}, "audit": {"by": "Sonnet 5.5", "verdict": "clean", "reason": "The `inspect` function (command 4) and `current-position-liberties.js` only parse the current board, list chains/liberties/empty regions, and apply one named move to report captures. The other commands played moves, replayed the move log to check the final board, and wrote the SGF and notes; none of it chose or ranked moves or looked ahead.", "stop": false}}